---
scope: src/tools/validate
status: aligned
description: Health-check tool that surfaces spec-layout drift — naming conflicts, orphaned research, missing or orphaned specs, broken relative links — so agents can fix it before the layout decays.
intent: >
  aide_validate is the layout health check. An agent calls it to find
  out whether a project's spec layout is internally consistent, and
  receives a findings list it can fix using scaffold and read. The tool
  detects drift in the spec layout itself, not in generated output —
  output drift is the QA agent's domain. Findings come in two
  confidence classes so the agent can triage: facts about the file
  system the tool can be certain about, and inferred signals that are
  useful but admit false positives. Silence on a clean project is the
  success state.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - A call with no path scans the whole project; a call with a path scans only that subtree. Scope is a parameter, not a filter applied after the fact, because QA and fix agents work one module at a time.
    - Findings separate the confidence class the agent acts on. Facts about the file system are surfaced as errors the agent can fix mechanically; inferred signals are surfaced as warnings the agent investigates before acting on.
    - Findings are reported as readable lines naming the issue class and offending path, not as a structured envelope the agent has to parse. A clean run returns a single explicit success line and nothing else.
    - Relative links inside spec bodies resolve against the spec's own directory. A link that does not resolve is a finding; wikilinks and external URLs are out of scope for this local, offline check.
  undesired:
    - One unreadable file aborts the whole scan. A single corrupt spec must not blind the agent to every other finding in the project.
    - Heuristic findings labeled with the same confidence as deterministic ones, so the agent acts on inferred signals with certainty they do not warrant.
    - A scoped run that emits findings outside the scope the caller passed. The whole point of scoping is keeping output focused on the module under review.
    - A clean project treated as a failure condition — empty output, an error, or a warning about nothing-to-validate. Silence on a clean project is the goal state, not a bug.
---
